ntinue to  be proud of the service of the very  brave men and women who stepped forward  in our armed forces and went to  afghanistan huge personal risk to  themselves we can be proud of that  commitment what i think we do now  is is redouble our efforts  diplomatically we  have been engaged in that country for a  long period of time i think we should  retain a commitment to afghanistan i  think it's in our national interest to  do so for the benefit of the afghan  population but also given the effort and  the sacrifice that we've invested i  think it would be morally reprehensible  and strategically naive to completely  abandon the country so i hope we'll see  a stepping up of statecraft if you like  and and governments uh across the world  but led by the british and american  governments  re-engaging having a pragmatic position  when it comes to the new administration  in uh afghanistan and seeing what can be  done to avert a humanitarian catastrophe  because that's what we're looking at if  we don't engage and if we don't try and  invest in that country given everything  that we put in over the previous  generation or so it's easy to give the  government a kicking and to say how  could they not have seen what was going  to happen but they rely on the top brass  and the chief of the general staff just  two weeks ago less than two weeks ago  was saying that we shouldn't give up on  the afghan security services he was very  wrong  yes he was no one person  is accountable for the strategic failure  that we've seen in afghanistan the most  catastrophic miscalculation that's taken  place in recent time about the ability  of the afghan government and the afghan  national security forces to withstand  the pressures that would inevitably come  from the taliban once the americans  departed by the way the manner of their  departure left much to be desired and  from the point at which that they left  the country in the dead of night i think  there was something of an inevitability  that the country would fall again to the  taliban  but this is happening on the  government's watch i listened to  president biden's speech with some  interest last night i don't think  history will judge kindly his handling  of the situation in recent times and  what i hope we will see  is an effort to engage regional partners  there's a hugely important role for  pakistan for iran and for others to play  in terms of seeking to mitigate the  worst of a humanitarian crisis and i  think whilst clearly we're not going to  be pulling military leavers in the very  near future to seek to affect the  situation in afghanistan there is other  influence that we can use albeit that  may be limited in terms of diplomatic  and economic leverage so i want to see a  redoubling of the international efforts  i want to see the british government  playing a leading role i want to see the  prime minister as the chair of the g7  engaging with partner nations to look at  the art of the possible what can be done  what pressure can be applied to the  taliban regime in kabul that doesn't  mean recognizing them formally but it is  in our interest to enter into dialogue  with them we're still in the process of  recovering british passport holders and  other entitled personnel to safety from  afghanistan that process will be made  much easier if we've got some sort of  diplomatic relationship so that work  needs to be happening right now how  do you feel  about um our politicians  engaging in dialogue with the taliban  who were people  that shot your friends and colleagues  well of course from a personal point of  view it is a particularly bitter pill to  have to swallow but we need to be  pragmatic about it there is a new norm  now in afghanistan and it is in our  country's national interest not to  abandon the country not to look away but  to remain engaged to look at what  diplomatic relations can be established  as i say that doesn't mean formally  recognizing the new administration  but we should be having dialogue with  them we should be looking to see  what humanitarian activities can be  taking place now and in the near future  we've got to have some sort of working  relationship we currently have a british  military operation underway to take  british personnel out of afghanistan  that will be made much easier if some  kind of agreement can be reached in the  short term to provide safe passage for  those who want to leave the country so  i'm obviously not particularly wild  about the fact that there does need to  be some contact with the taliban given  you know all of the difficulties we've  obviously experienced with them in the  past but it is in our national interest  to do so and i hope very much that  through the foreign office and through  other channels conversations are taking  place to minimize the risks to our uk  forces who are currently conducting a  difficult and dangerous operation and  we're already starting to think about  what activities can be put in place to  avert a humanitarian catastrophe because  if that doesn't happen we will see very  significant suffering and it's the  afghan people and other countries in the  region who will suffer as a consequence  okay for now thank you
 

